{
  "id": "ch-parametric",
  "chapterNumber": "9",
  "title": {
    "en": "Parametric &amp; Polar",
    "ar": null
  },
  "content": {
    "blocks": [
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "body": {
          "en": "Not every curve is a function \\( y=f(x) \\). Parametric equations trace a path through time, and polar coordinates describe position by distance and angle — both unlock curves that loop, spiral, and cross themselves.",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "thm",
        "label": {
          "en": "▲ Theorem 9.1 — Parametric calculus",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p>For a curve \\( \\big(x(t),\\,y(t)\\big) \\), the slope and arc length are</p>\\[ \\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{dy/dt}{dx/dt},\\qquad L=\\int_a^b\\sqrt{\\Big(\\tfrac{dx}{dt}\\Big)^2+\\Big(\\tfrac{dy}{dt}\\Big)^2}\\;dt. \\]",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "thm",
        "label": {
          "en": "▲ Theorem 9.2 — Polar coordinates &amp; area",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p>With \\( x=r\\cos\\theta,\\ y=r\\sin\\theta \\), the area swept by a polar curve is</p>\\[ A=\\frac12\\int_\\alpha^\\beta r^2\\,d\\theta. \\]",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "body": {
          "en": "Drag the trace slider to watch each curve drawn by its parameter — the indigo portion is traced so far, the amber dot is the current point.",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "explorer",
